# Force LF line endings for all text files — prevents Windows CRLF corruption
# of multi-byte Unicode characters (em-dashes, curly quotes etc.) in Python source.
# Python 3.12 on the server is strict about byte sequences; CRLF conversion breaks them.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ── BAT-CRLF-1 (26 Aug 2026) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXCEPTION to the eol=lf rule above, and it must stay an exception.
# The rule above is right for everything that reaches the Linux server. It is
# WRONG for Windows scripts: cmd.exe reads a .bat byte by byte and expects CRLF.
# A caret line-continuation followed by a bare LF does NOT continue the line --
# cmd mangles the command, throws, and the window closes instantly. That is the
# 26 Aug "add_secret.bat flickered on and off": 15 caret continuations feeding a
# PowerShell block, LF endings, no pause, nothing readable before it vanished.
# ROTATE_SECRETS.bat carried the same latent fault (5 carets), and the deploy
# lane bats would have inherited it on any fresh checkout.
# CLASS: a script's line endings belong to the interpreter that reads it, never
# to the repo's default. Guarded by scripts/check_bat_crlf.py, ledger RG-0192.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Explicitly enforce LF for the files we deploy to the Linux server
bea_main.py text eol=lf
marketsquare.html text eol=lf
marketsquare_admin.html text eol=lf
CHANGELOG.md text eol=lf
STATUS.md text eol=lf
AGENT_BRIEFING.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE.md text eol=lf

# Binary files — never touch line endings
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
